## 2. Terminating Processes: `kill` vs. `kill -9`

If a process consumes too many resources or is identified as malicious (e.g., a reverse shell backdoor), you must terminate it.

| Command | Signal | Description |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **`kill [PID]`** | **SIGTERM (15)** | Sends a **SIGTERM (polite request to stop)**. The process can catch this signal, save its current state, clean up temp files, and exit gracefully. |
| **`kill -9 [PID]`** | **SIGKILL (9)** | Sends a **SIGKILL (forceful termination)**. The kernel immediately kills the process, bypassing any cleanup operations. Use this only when a process is frozen or refuses to terminate. |

> [!WARNING]
> Force-killing a database or active service with `kill -9` can lead to data corruption since the process is not allowed to close open file handlers properly.

